---
/* Evaluate abap2UI5 - the page for the people who approve it: IT management,
 * architecture boards, product owners, SAP Basis, internal audit. The
 * questions a review asks, in the order it asks them.
 *
 * THE SECTION IDS ARE ADDRESSES. /docs/evaluate redirects here with its
 * #fragment, and the framework's README links #what-you-keep and
 * #when-to-use-it-and-when-not; test/site.test.mjs names all eight, so a
 * renamed id is a failing build, not a dead link. */
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import Stats from "../components/Stats.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import CostCalculator from "../components/CostCalculator.astro";

const DOCS = "https://abap2ui5.github.io/docs/";
const PLAYGROUND = "https://abap2ui5.github.io/playground/";
const GITHUB = "https://github.com/abap2UI5/abap2UI5";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const useIt = [
  ["The team is ABAP.", "No JavaScript, no UI5 tooling, no Business Application Studio to set up. An app is one class, written, debugged, transported and unit-tested like every other class."],
  ["The system is older than RAP.", "NetWeaver 7.02 to 7.4x have no RAP and a Fiori stack that is expensive to add. abap2UI5 runs there from the same source it runs on ABAP Cloud, so an app written on 7.31 today moves to S/4HANA unchanged."],
  ["The screen is yours, not SAP's.", "Tools, cockpits, monitors, admin consoles, anything that used to be a report or a module pool. The moment a screen is a dashboard with a chart and a custom form, a Fiori Elements template becomes the thing you fight."],
  ["You do not want a deployment pipeline.", "No BSP upload, no approuter, no MTA. The ABAP system serves the frontend; an abapGit pull is the installation."],
  ["You replace SAP GUI screens one at a time.", "Selection screens, lists and transactions become browser apps without rewriting the logic behind them."],
];
const useRap = [
  ["The service is the product.", "Other consumers, a mobile app, an integration, SAP's own apps, need the OData service anyway. RAP gives you the service and the UI from one model; abap2UI5 gives you a UI and no service."],
  ["The screen is a standard floorplan and should stay one.", "List report, object page, worklist, with SAP's draft handling and flexible column layout as SAP ships and maintains them. Fiori Elements tracks every UI5 release for you; a hand-built screen is yours to keep current."],
  ["The data is large and lives behind paging.", "Fiori Elements pages, sorts and filters through OData on the server. In abap2UI5 the table a view binds travels with the model; thousands of rows are routine, millions are the app's job to page."],
  ["Offline, or a native mobile SDK, is a requirement.", "abap2UI5 is a roundtrip to the ABAP system on every interaction. It has a mobile shell for camera, barcode and location; it has no offline store."],
  ["A frontend team should own the frontend.", "The app is one ABAP class; that is the point, and it means there is no frontend project for a separate team to own, and no place for heavy client-side behavior."],
  ["The organization has standardized on it.", "A clean-core guideline that names RAP as the extensibility model is a decision already taken. abap2UI5 passes the ABAP Cloud checks, but it is not SAP's programming model, and a review board will say so."],
];
const costCompare = [
  ["Skills", "ABAP, and a reading knowledge of the UI5 control API", "ABAP, CDS, RAP, annotations, and someone who can debug the Fiori Elements template"],
  ["First screen", "One class, minutes", "Data model, behavior definition, service, Fiori project, deploy: a day when everything is set up"],
  ["Custom UI", "Any UI5 control, 1:1, from ABAP", "Extension points; past them, a freestyle UI5 project"],
  ["Upgrade", "The framework is a transport; the public API is held by a snapshot and deprecations are tracked", "SAP's; the template follows the UI5 release"],
  ["Reuse by other consumers", "None, there is no service", "The OData service, by anything"],
  ["Runs on", "NetWeaver 7.02 to ABAP Cloud, UI5 1.71 to 2.x", "7.5x and later for RAP, ABAP Cloud for the full model"],
  ["License", "MIT, no per-user cost", "Part of the platform license"],
];
const keep = [
  ["What an app is", "An ABAP class"],
  ["Where a change is made", "In the development system, in ADT or SE80"],
  ["How a change travels", "A transport, like any class"],
  ["How a change is reviewed", "As a code diff: in abapGit, a pull request or the transport"],
  ["What a debugger shows", "Your code"],
  ["What happens if the framework goes away", "The classes stay; they are plain ABAP in your system"],
];
const strings = [
  ["No license, now or later", "MIT. No license key, no subscription, no fee per user, per app or per system - and nothing to renew."],
  ["Nothing to sign up for", "The playground runs in the browser with no account. The framework is an abapGit pull; there is no trial period and no demo to book."],
  ["No runtime in between", "An app is a class and runs as ABAP. The framework itself is ABAP source in your system, readable to the last line."],
  ["No extra platform", "No BTP subscription, no middleware, no server of its own. One ICF node on the system you already run."],
  ["The tools you already use", "ADT, SE80, abapGit, ATC, abaplint, ABAP Unit, the debugger and your AI assistant work on an app as on any other class."],
  ["Every UI5 control", "Any control of the UI5 release your system runs, 1:1 from ABAP - the whole library, not a selection."],
  ["Free to leave", "The apps stay ordinary classes. A build renamed into your own namespace is one workflow run, and the source is yours to fork."],
  ["Built in the open", "Every issue, every release, the backlog and every test that holds the code are public on GitHub."],
];
const governance = [
  ["lock", "Authentication is SAP's", "No user store, no token, no session concept of its own. A request that reaches an app has passed SAP logon."],
  ["key", "Authorization is two gates", "The authorization object on the ICF node, as for any service, and the app's own authority checks in its code, the same way a transaction or a report does them. The framework adds no central check on purpose."],
  ["db", "Data changes are your code's", "An app posts a document by calling the same BAPIs, function modules or RAP actions any other ABAP program calls, so change documents, application logs and the authority checks behind them apply unchanged."],
  ["log", "Every roundtrip can be logged", "A monitor interface receives, for each request, the user, the app, the event, the timings and any error. It is off until an installation switches it on."],
  ["leaf", "Clean core", "abap2UI5 uses released APIs only, passes the ABAP Cloud checks and runs in ABAP Cloud, on S/4HANA and on BTP, from the same source that runs on NetWeaver 7.02. It modifies nothing in the SAP standard."],
];
const objections = [
  ["It is an open-source project. What if the maintainers stop?", `The installed version keeps running, the source is in your system in full, readable and changeable, and the apps stay ordinary ABAP classes. Nothing is called at runtime that is not in your system, apart from the UI5 library, which you can host yourself. A build renamed into your own namespace is one workflow run (<a href="${DOCS}advanced/renaming">Namespaces, Renaming</a>).`],
  ["Is this another UI5 wrapper that will not survive the next UI5 release?", `The frontend is UI5 itself, not a copy of it, and every merge is run in the browser on the oldest supported release and on UI5 2.x. Releases are tagged and listed (<a href="${DOCS}resources/changelog">Release Notes</a>); what is on its way out is announced and tracked until removal (<a href="${DOCS}resources/deprecations">Deprecations</a>), and the public API apps use is held by a snapshot, so a change to it fails the framework's own pull request.`],
  ["Every click is a roundtrip. Is that fast enough?", `A roundtrip is one HTTP POST carrying only what changed, served by the ABAP stack that already serves everything else. For the screens this page is about, worklists, forms, cockpits, it is the same round trip a SAP GUI screen makes, in a browser. What it is not built for is a real-time collaborative editor or an app that holds its state offline (<a href="${DOCS}configuration/performance">Performance</a>).`],
  ["Our clean-core guideline names RAP. Where does this leave us?", "Side by side. abap2UI5 passes the ABAP Cloud checks and modifies nothing in the standard, so it is clean core in the technical sense, but it is not SAP's programming model. The usual outcome is a scope: standard transactional screens in RAP, the tools around them in abap2UI5, both in one launchpad."],
  ["Who is accountable when something breaks?", `Your developer, for the app, the same as for any class in the system; the community, for the framework, on a best-effort basis; a commercial partner, if you sign one. Security reports are acknowledged within three business days (<a href="${DOCS}configuration/security">Security</a>).`],
  ["Is anybody actually running this in production?", `Yes, see below. And every release is tested against Standard ABAP and ABAP Cloud, downported and linted against NetWeaver 7.02, and run in the browser on the oldest and the newest supported UI5 release (<a href="${GITHUB}/blob/main/docs/verified-on.md">verified on</a>).`],
];
const companies = [
  ["Emineo", "https://emineo.ch/", "ABAP 7.57", "Apps for EWM and PP, running on desktop and mobile devices"],
  ["Swiss Krono", "https://www.swisskrono.com/", "ABAP 7.55", "Apps for EWM, running on mobile devices"],
  ["Nuve Platform", "https://www.nuveplatform.com", "Integration", "On-demand SAP systems with automated abap2UI5 setups"],
  ["Logali Group", "https://logaligroup.com", "Training", "Webinars and certifications for abap2UI5"],
];
const references = [
  ["2026", "UI5 in ABAP Cloud, without RAP or Fiori Elements", "Decabase Blog", "https://blog.decabase.com/ui5-in-abap-cloud-without-rap-or-fiori-elements-4e8a70d961c3"],
  ["2026", "Fiori-type apps built 100% with ABAP", "Logali Group", "https://logaligroup.com/magia-en-el-servidor-local-apps-tipo-fiori-creadas-100-con-codigo-abap/"],
  ["2025", "Featured on SAP Developer News", "YouTube", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vKrpkDe2mkU&list=PL6RpkC85SLQAVBSQXN9522_1jNvPavBgg&t=90s"],
  ["2025", "Webinar on Launchpad Integration", "YouTube", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t5g3F3PHsbw&list=PLLpkZ_86h4quGSfsjohDHt9CrpjXdeA1P"],
  ["2024", "Featured on SAP Developer News", "YouTube", "https://youtu.be/7n16u-Rx8IY?t=7"],
  ["2024", "Webinar on developing UI5 apps with abap2UI5", "YouTube", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0izPA2xrPPI"],
  ["2023", "Showcased at SAP TechEd 2023", "YouTube", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kLbF0ooStZs&t=3052s"],
  ["2023", "Part of the SAP Developer Code Challenge", "SAP Community", "https://groups.community.sap.com/t5/application-development/sap-developer-code-challenge-open-source-abap-week-2/m-p/260727#M1372"],
];
const addons = [
  ["selection-screen", "Selection screens and variants"],
  ["table-maintenance", "Table maintenance in your browser"],
  ["layout-management", "Customizable table and form layouts with persistent variants"],
  ["popups", "Value help, search help, transport selection"],
  ["admin-cockpit", "Usage, performance, errors and a security traffic light for an installation"],
  ["launchpad-kpi", "KPIs of abap2UI5 apps on the Fiori launchpad"],
  ["abap-cloud-gui", "Apps written like a classic report - selection screen, WRITE list, ALV - on ABAP Cloud and down to 7.02"],
  ["http-connector · rfc-connector", "Call abap2UI5 apps remotely over HTTP or RFC"],
];
const week = [
  ["Pick one real screen", "One your team has to build this quarter — not a demo."],
  ["Build it in a development system", "With the people who will maintain it, in ADT or SE80."],
  ["Review it like any ABAP", "Read the class, transport it, run ATC on it, check the authorizations it asks for."],
  ["Count", "The files, the systems and the tools involved, and the time until a user can click it."],
  ["Hold it against the evidence", "This page against the security model and the verified-on page, with the skepticism you would apply to any claim — then decide from the screen."],
];
const doors = [
  ["play", "Ten minutes, nothing installed", "Open the playground and change a running app, or browse the sample catalog for the screen closest to yours.", `${PLAYGROUND}samples/`, "Playground"],
  ["code", "One afternoon, one developer", "Send them the Quickstart — abapGit pull, ICF node, first app. The tutorial takes the same app to transport and unit tests.", `${DOCS}get_started/quickstart`, "Quickstart"],
  ["chat", "One question", "Ask it in the Slack channel or as a GitHub issue, where the people who run it in production answer.", "https://abapgit.slack.com/join/shared_invite/zt-46tqufaht-QlrxTzlDqlx85CWbeUnOqg#/shared-invite/email", "Community"],
  ["partner", "A partner", "The companies on Who Uses abap2UI5? offer implementation, workshops and support agreements.", `${DOCS}resources/who_uses`, "Partners"],
];
---
<Page
  title="Evaluate abap2UI5 · for the people who approve it"
  description="abap2UI5 for the people who approve a tool rather than build with it - what problem it answers, when it is the right choice and when RAP is, what your organization keeps, where it runs, what it costs, the objections a review board raises, and how to test it on one real screen in a week."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">For <b>IT management, architecture boards, product owners, SAP Basis</b> and <b>internal audit</b></p>
        <h1>Evaluate <em>abap2UI5</em> with the questions a review asks</h1>
        <p class="lede">In the order a review asks them, each with the page that carries the detail. A developer who wants to see it run is better served by the <a href={`${DOCS}get_started/quickstart`}>Quickstart</a>; send them there and read on.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={PLAYGROUND}>Try it in the playground</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={`${DOCS}get_started/about`}>Read the technical picture</a>
        </div>
        <p class="note">Nothing to install, no trial license, no sales call — the playground runs in your browser.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="window">
        <div class="window-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>an abap2UI5 app · one ABAP class</span></div>
        <img src={`${base}walkthrough-preview.webp`} alt="An abap2UI5 app: a selection form above a table of invoices, with a dialog editing one row" width="1000" height="598" fetchpriority="high">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Stats />

  <section class="section" id="the-problem-it-answers" aria-labelledby="h-problem">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The problem it answers</p>
      <h2 id="h-problem">Every SAP system has screens nobody shipped</h2>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The cockpit for one team, the monitor for one process, the selection screen with a result list that used to be a report with an ALV. They are not standard floorplans, so Fiori Elements does not cover them; they are not worth a frontend project, an OData service and a deployment pipeline, so they stay in SAP GUI, in a spreadsheet, or on paper. And on a system older than S/4HANA, RAP is not there to build them with at all.</p>
          <p>abap2UI5 is the shorter path for exactly these screens. An app is one ABAP class, written in ADT or SE80, transported like any other class, and it renders as a UI5 app in the browser, in the Fiori launchpad, or on a mobile device. There is no service, no frontend project and nothing to deploy. The rest of this page is what that costs and what it does not.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cards stack">
          <div class="card"><Icon name="gauge" /><h3>The cockpit for one team</h3><p>Too specific for a standard app, too small for a project.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><Icon name="monitor" /><h3>The monitor for one process</h3><p>Today a report someone runs by hand, or a spreadsheet.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><Icon name="list" /><h3>The selection screen with a list</h3><p>The ALV report, as a browser app, logic unchanged.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="more">→ <a href={`${DOCS}get_started/about`}>In a Nutshell</a> is the technical picture of how the framework works, for the architect who wants to see the roundtrip.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt" id="when-to-use-it-and-when-not" aria-labelledby="h-fit">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">When to use it, and when not</p>
      <h2 id="h-fit">One way to put a UI5 interface on ABAP — not the only one</h2>
      <p class="intro">RAP with Fiori Elements is another, freestyle UI5 with OData a third, and all three run side by side in the same launchpad. Written so that a team can disagree with a specific line rather than with a feeling.</p>
      <div class="fit">
        <div class="yes">
          <h3>Use abap2UI5 when</h3>
          <ul class="reasons">{useIt.map(([b, t]) => <li><b>{b}</b>{t}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
        <div class="no">
          <h3>Use RAP and Fiori Elements when</h3>
          <ul class="reasons">{useRap.map(([b, t]) => <li><b>{b}</b>{t}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="callout"><b>The common answer is not either.</b> Standard transactional screens as Fiori Elements, the tools and cockpits around them as abap2UI5, both as tiles in the same launchpad with navigation between them. What it costs either way:</p>
      <div class="table">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col" class="us">abap2UI5</th><th scope="col">RAP + Fiori Elements</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{costCompare.map(([k, a, b]) => <tr><th scope="row">{k}</th><td class="us">{a}</td><td>{b}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="more">→ <a href={`${DOCS}advanced/insights/30-on-stack-or-side-by-side`}>On-Stack or Side-by-Side</a> and <a href={`${DOCS}advanced/insights/31-one-app-many-systems`}>One App, Many Systems</a> are the deployment scenarios in detail.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="what-you-keep" aria-labelledby="h-keep">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">What you keep</p>
      <h2 id="h-keep">An abap2UI5 app is ABAP code, and governs itself like it</h2>
      <p class="intro">The decision everything else follows from: one ABAP class per app, no generated artifacts, no tables that describe a screen.</p>
      <div class="table">
        <table class="two">
          <tbody>{keep.map(([k, a]) => <tr><th scope="row">{k}</th><td>{a}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="intro">So abap2UI5 needs no rule of its own. Every rule that already applies to ABAP — naming conventions, code review, ATC, the four-eyes principle on transports — applies to an app without any change. A system closed for repository changes is closed for abap2UI5 apps.</p>
      <div class="cards five">
        {governance.map(([icon, h, t]) => <div class="card"><Icon name={icon} /><h3>{h}</h3><p>{t}</p></div>)}
      </div>
      <p class="callout"><b>The price is that someone has to write ABAP.</b> There is no click-together designer for business users, on purpose: every app stays one kind of thing, a class, governed by the rules you already have.</p>
      <p class="more">→ More on <a href={`${DOCS}configuration/security`}>Security</a> and <a href={`${DOCS}configuration/authorization`}>Authorization</a>, and the <a href={`${GITHUB}/blob/main/docs/security.md`}>security model</a> a security review starts from: what the framework guarantees, and which test holds the code to each guarantee.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section start" id="no-strings-attached" aria-labelledby="h-strings">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">No strings attached</p>
      <h2 id="h-strings">Nothing to buy, nothing to renew, nothing between you and your code</h2>
      <p class="intro">What a review can check in minutes, and what stays true after the decision.</p>
      <ul class="strings">{strings.map(([b, t]) => <li><b>{b}</b><span>{t}</span></li>)}</ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt" id="where-it-runs" aria-labelledby="h-runs">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Where it runs</p>
      <h2 id="h-runs">One HTTP endpoint, and nothing else listens</h2>
      <p class="intro">App code never leaves the system; the browser receives the view and the data the app chose to bind. No middleware, no extra server, no BTP subscription, no frontend deployment.</p>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="box">
          <h3>Browser</h3>
          <ol><li>Generic UI5 shell<small>loaded once, the same for every app; no app code of its own</small></li></ol>
        </div>
        <div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <div class="box server">
          <h3>SAP system — the only server</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>ICF node → abap2UI5 handler → your app class</li>
            <li>SAP logon, authorization objects, sy-uname</li>
            <li>App state in one table, per user</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="split">
        <dl class="facts-list">
          <dt>Systems</dt>
          <dd><ul class="chips"><li>S/4HANA Public Cloud</li><li>BTP ABAP Environment</li><li>S/4HANA Private Cloud &amp; on-premise</li><li>NetWeaver 7.02 or later</li></ul></dd>
          <dt>UI5</dt>
          <dd>The frontend is UI5 itself, 1.71 to 2.x. By default it loads from the public CDN; a productive installation points it at the UI5 the system already ships, which is what a system without internet access uses.</dd>
          <dt>Where an app appears</dt>
          <dd><ul class="chips"><li>Browser tab</li><li>Fiori launchpad tile</li><li>SAP Build Work Zone</li><li>Microsoft Teams</li><li>Microsoft Excel</li><li>iOS &amp; Android</li><li>Inside your own UI5 app</li></ul></dd>
        </dl>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 class="first">What Basis does — all of it</h3>
          <ol class="steps-basis">
            <li>Pull the package with abapGit</li>
            <li>Create the ICF node and put an authorization object on it</li>
            <li>Change the four production defaults in the user exit: UI5 source, error details, the Content Security Policy, the node authorization</li>
            <li>Optionally switch on the monitor</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="after">No job to schedule and no schema to migrate on upgrade.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="more">→ More on <a href={`${DOCS}configuration/productive_usage`}>Productive Usage</a>, <a href={`${DOCS}configuration/ui5_versions`}>UI5 Versions</a>, <a href={`${DOCS}configuration/performance`}>Performance</a> and <a href={`${DOCS}integration/`}>Integration</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="what-it-costs" aria-labelledby="h-cost">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">What it costs</p>
      <h2 id="h-cost">No license fee, now or later — the work is what you pay for</h2>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="price">
          <span class="big">0 <small>license fees</small></span>
          <ul>
            <li>MIT licensed, commercial use included</li>
            <li>No license key, no subscription</li>
            <li>No per-user, per-system or per-app fee</li>
            <li>Runs on the ABAP stack and the UI5 version you already have</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The apps built with it are standard UI5 freestyle apps, licensed the way your organization licenses every other UI5 app.</p>
          <p><b>What is not free is the work:</b> an ABAP developer's time to write the screen, and the evenings of the volunteers who maintain the framework. Support is the community's, on GitHub issues and Slack, on a best-effort basis; there is no vendor SLA to buy from the project. Companies and freelancers offer paid implementation, training and support agreements around it.</p>
          <p class="more">→ More on the <a href={`${DOCS}resources/license`}>License</a> and <a href={`${DOCS}resources/support`}>Support</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h3 class="calc-title">Work it out for your landscape</h3>
      <p class="intro">Set the sliders to your landscape, tick your systems and your support, pick a currency, press Calculate.</p>
      <CostCalculator />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt" id="the-objections" aria-labelledby="h-obj">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The objections</p>
      <h2 id="h-obj">The questions a review board raises, with the honest answer to each</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        {objections.map(([q, a], i) => <details open={i === 0}><summary>{q}</summary><p set:html={a} /></details>)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="who-runs-it" aria-labelledby="h-who">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Who runs it</p>
      <h2 id="h-who">In production, on desktop and on the warehouse floor</h2>
      <p class="intro">Companies run abap2UI5 apps for EWM and PP on desktop and mobile devices, consultancies offer workshops and certifications for it, and it has been featured in SAP Developer News and at SAP TechEd.</p>
      <ul class="logos">{companies.map(([name, href, tag, text]) => <li><a href={href}>{name}</a><small>{tag}</small><span>{text}</span></li>)}</ul>
      <h3 class="refs-title">Talks, articles and videos</h3>
      <ul class="refs">{references.map(([year, title, where, href]) => <li><time>{year}</time><a href={href}>{title}</a><small>{where}</small></li>)}</ul>
      <p class="more">→ <a href={`${DOCS}resources/who_uses`}>Who Uses abap2UI5?</a> and <a href={`${DOCS}resources/references`}>References</a> have the full lists - both are open for your own row.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt" id="add-ons" aria-labelledby="h-addons">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Add-ons</p>
      <h2 id="h-addons">The framework stays small; the building blocks are ready</h2>
